## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical understanding of simple rate limiting, memory bounds, and concurrency in a real service.

## Key Concepts

- In-memory data structures for per-key state
- Time-window calculations and pruning
- Concurrency and process-safety; scalability trade-offs

## Code Example

```javascript
const buckets = new Map();

function limit(req, res, next){
  const key = req.headers['x-api-key'];
  if(!key) return res.status(400).send('API key required');
  const now = Date.now();
  const arr = buckets.get(key) || [];
  // prune
  while (arr.length && now - arr[0] > 10 * 60 * 1000) arr.shift();
  if (arr.length >= 100) return res.status(429).send('Too many requests');
  arr.push(now);
  buckets.set(key, arr);
  next();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for a multi-instance deployment?
- What are the limitations of in-memory rate limiting?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical ability to diagnose multi-tenant feature-flag issues in production using Kubernetes, Redis, and tracing. It probes understanding of cache stampede, per-tenant routing, rollout safety, and latency requirements.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant feature flags and cache invalidation
- Canary rollouts and rollback safety
- Redis keyspace examination and TTL semantics
- Distributed tracing for tenant-aware requests
- Latency SLO verification under load

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo canary rollout flow
if (flagEnabledForTenant(tenant)) {
  routeToTenantService(tenant);
} else {
  routeToFallback(tenant);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for 10k tenants with dynamic flag sets?
- What rollback criteria would you codify (latency, error rate, traffic shift)?
